In C++, you can use a lambda function or a function object to encapsulate the code that needs to be executed. By passing the same lambda or function object to multiple variables, you can run the same code inline on those variables. Here's a concise example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

void printValue(int value) {
    std::cout << "Value: " << value << std::endl;
}

int main() {
    int x = 10, y = 20, z = 30;
    auto print = [](int value) { printValue(value); };

    print(x);
    print(y);
    print(z);

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the lambda function `[](int value) { printValue(value); }` is defined and then passed to the `print` variable. This allows you to run the same code inline on multiple variables `x`, `y`, and `z` by calling the `print` function with each variable as an argument.

